A||||3||MooCow||Engine Question!!!!!!!!||||5||
Z||000000||MooCow||02-13-2000||04:41 PM||||If I find a Fiero of any year that I like at an auction in nice shape with power everything can I switch the stock engine for a 3.8 or 3.4 engine??  Does the year on the Fiero matter?  Does it matter if it is a 4 or 6 cylinder Fiero??  Also about how much would the engine cost installed?? (not by myself because I know nothing about engines)<BR>Thanks Ken||||reg||5
Z||000001||GT Bastard||02-13-2000||08:46 PM||||You'll want to make sure you're not getting an '84 Fiero or you'll need to swap engine cradles with an '85 or later.  I CAN be done, but if you're not going to do it yourself, you'd better have a knowledgable mechanic and plent of money to burn because this is NOT a direct replacement.  The 3.4 would be much easier though.... There's been plenty of info posted about this, so you'll find plenty of info on this board if you do a search.  later....||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||02-13-2000||09:09 PM||||Buying an 88 will save you the cost of a new flywheel if you do a pushrod 3.4 swap using the Fiero manual trans.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||02-13-2000||10:36 PM||||I regards to 3.8L there are kits so you don't have to change the cradle. There is a place by me that does 3.8L conversions. Average cost is $4000 - $5000 Not including Engine. He sells kits for the cradle for $1800 As for 3.4L all Fiero 2.8L parts are interchangeable to the FWD block but on the RWD (Camero/Firebird) you have to move the starter. The conversion to a 3.4L at the place is $2500 - $3000. This includes the FWD long block. If you go to the 3.4L get an '85 thru '88. The V-6 ECM will work of you use the exsisting fiero fuel and ignition systems. You do need to change the fuel regulator to a variable one. The '84 does not have wire harness support for a V-6 so the chassis harness would need to be replaced or modified. The 3.4L bolts up to the trans, The 3.8 has different parts which are included in the kit.<BR>||||reg||1
